'''Betula''' (bet̮′ū-lä̤), ''n.'' [L., the birch, also spelled ''betulla'' (> It. ''betula'', ''betulla'', also ''bedello'', = Pg. ''betulla'' = Sp. ''abedul'' = F. dim. ''bouleau''); cf. Corn. ''betho'', ''bezo'' = Bret. ''bezo'' = W. ''bedw'' = Gael. ''beth'' = Ir. ''beth'', ''beit'', the birch.] A genus of hardy trees or shrubs, natives of the north temperate and arctic regions; the birches. {{smaller|It is the type of the order ''Betulaceæ'', and is distinguished from the accompanying genus ''Alnus'' by a difference of habit and by its winged nutlet. There are about 30 species of ''Betula'', of which 10 are North American.}}

'''Betulaceæ''' (bet̮-ū-lā′sē-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Betula'' + ''-aceæ''.] A natural order of apetalous dicotyledonous trees and shrubs, of which ''Betula'' is the typical genus, and containing
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Betula.''<br>''a'', branch of ''B. pumila'', with male and female aments; ''b'', a single scale of fertile ament, with fruit. (From Le Maout and Decaisne’s “Traité général de Botanique.”)}}}}
besides this only the genus ''Alnus'', with 60 species belonging to the two genera. See cut under ''alder''.

