''tween'' the eyes; ''between'' Washington and Philadelphia; the prisoner was placed ''between'' two policemen.
{{smaller block|<poem>The sea
Swallows him with his host, but them lets pass
As on dry land, ''between'' two crystal walls.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 197.}}}}
'''2.''' In intermediate relation to, as regards time, quantity, or degree: as, it occurred ''between'' his incoming and outgoing; a baronet is ''between'' a knight and a baron; they cost ''between'' $5 and $6 each; ''between'' 12 and 1 o’clock.
{{smaller block|<poem>Bolus arrived, and gave a doubtful tap,
''Between'' a single and a double rap.</poem>
{{right|''Colman'', Broad Grins.}}
<poem>Her lips to mine how often hath she joined,
''Between'' each kiss her oaths of true love swearing!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Pass. Pilgrim, vii.}}}}
'''3.''' In the mutual relations of: as, discord exists ''between'' the two families.
{{smaller block|Friendship requires that it be ''between'' two at least.
{{right|''South.''}}
An intestine struggle, open or secret, ''between'' authority and liberty. {{right|''Hume'', Essays, v.}}
The war ''between'' Castile and Portugal had come to a close; the factions of the Spanish nobles were for the most part quelled. {{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 26.}}
Differences of relative position can be known only through differences ''between'' the states of consciousness accompanying the disclosure of the positions.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Psychol., § 93.}}}}
'''4.''' From one to another of, as in the exchange of actions or intercourse.
{{smaller block|If things should go so ''between'' them.
{{right|''Bacon'', Hist. of Hen. VII.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>Thus graceless holds he disputation
’''Tween'' frozen conscience and hot-burning will.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 247.}}}}
{{smaller block|France has been the interpreter ''between'' England and mankind.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Horace Walpole.}}}}
'''5.''' In the joint interest or possession of: as, they own the property ''between'' them.
{{smaller block|There is ''between'' us one common name and appellation.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Castor and Pollux with only one soul ''between'' them.
{{right|''Locke.''}}}}
'''6.''' By the action, power, or effort of one or both of.
{{smaller block|<poem>Unless you send some present help,
''Between'' them they will kill the conjurer.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., v. 1.}}}}
'''7.''' In regard to the respective natures or qualities of: as, to distinguish ''between'' right and wrong.
{{smaller block|There is an essential difference ''between'' a land of which we can trace the gradual formation from the sixth century onwards and a land whose name is not heard of till the eleventh century.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Eng. Towns, p. 120.}}}}
'''8.''' In regard to one or the other of: as, to choose ''between'' two things.
{{smaller block|''Between'' two dogs, which hath the deeper mouth?
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., ii. 4.}}}}
