'''bevel''' (bev′el), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Formerly also ''bevell'', as a term of heraldry ''bevil, bevile'', < OF. *''bevel'' or *''buvel'' (not recorded), mod. F. ''biveau'', also spelled ''beveau, buveau, beuveau, beauveau'', etc. (cf. Sp. ''baivel''), bevel; origin unknown.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' The obliquity or inclination of a particular surface of a solid body to another surface of the same body; the angle contained by two adjacent sides of anything, as of a timber used in ship-building. When this angle is acute it is called an ''under bevel'' (or ''beveling''), and when
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Mechanics’ Bevels.}}}}
obtuse a ''standing bevel''.—'''2.''' An instrument used by mechanics for drawing angles and for adjusting the abutting surfaces of work to the same inclination. {{smaller|It consists of two limbs jointed together, one called the stock or handle and the other the blade; the latter is movable on a pivot at the joint, and can be adjusted so as to include any angle between it and the stock. The blade is often curved on the edge to}}

readings.c3s4:
suit the sweep of an arch or vault. See ''bevel-square.'' '''3.''' A piece of type-metal nearly type-high, with a beveled edge, used by stereotypers to form the flange on the sides of the plates. ''Worcester.'' —'''4.''' Same as ''bevel-angle.''—'''5.''' In ''her.'', an angular break in any right line.

'''II.''' ''a.'' Having the form of a bevel; aslant; sloping; out of the perpendicular; not upright: used figuratively by Shakspere.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Heraldic Bevel. (See ''beveled'', 3.)}}}}
{{smaller block|I may be straight though they themselves be ''bevel.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, cxxi.}}
Their houses are very ill built, the walls ''bevel'', without one right angle in any apartment.
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver’s Travels, iii. 2.}}}}

